# Reference template — the easiest way to create .env is:
#
#   bash setup.sh
#
# That script auto-detects the current directory, picks a device name from this
# computer's hostname, and generates a per-install login — writing all of it to
# .env for you.  If you prefer to do it by hand, copy this file and edit the two
# REQUIRED values below plus the login:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT must be the absolute WSL2/Linux path to the pipeline/
# subdirectory inside this repo (not the repo root itself).
# Windows example (WSL2): /mnt/<drive>/ODIN/odin-demo/pipeline
# Linux / macOS example:  /home/user/odin-demo/pipeline

ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT=/path/to/odin-demo/pipeline

# ── PATHOGEN LIST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which organisms get a "Priority pathogen group" in Enlighten. The demo tree keeps
# no copy under input_sheets/: the stack reads the deployment profile's file in
# place, so for the demo set this to <ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT>/deployments/mobile_lab_2025/
# pathogens.yaml (setup.sh writes it for you). For a field data folder created by
# init_pipeline_tree.sh leave it unset — that folder has its own
# input_sheets/pathogens.yaml, which is the default ODIN reads.
ODIN_PATHOGENS_FILE=/path/to/odin-demo/pipeline/deployments/mobile_lab_2025/pathogens.yaml

# Optional overlay on the column descriptions written into every Enlighten feather (the
# bundled texts are odin_pipeline's pipeline/config/dataset_columns.yaml; same layout, only
# the entries to change). Default: $ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT/config/dataset_columns.yaml if present.
# ODIN_DATASET_COLUMNS_FILE=/path/to/dataset_columns.yaml

# ── DEVICE IDENTITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# setup.sh fills this in from your hostname; set it yourself only if you skipped
# setup.sh or want a friendlier name.
#
# Name identifying this computer/operator.  REQUIRED — there is no default.
# It is stamped into created_by/updated_by on every row and into sync export
# filenames, which is how data from different machines is told apart when merging.
# Keep it stable: changing it makes this one machine look like two different
# devices once data is merged.
# Set it HERE rather than only in the Settings UI — .env lives on the host, so the
# value survives the container being recreated.  (The container's own hostname is a
# throwaway ID that changes each time, which is why it is not used as a fallback:
# it would silently scatter one machine's rows across several phantom devices.)
# Required for correct attribution rather than for startup: the app still runs if
# you leave it unset, but rows carry NULL and the Sync page warns you.
ODIN_DEVICE_NAME=my-laptop

# ── FRONTEND LOGIN (HTTP Basic Auth) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The frontend requires this username/password before showing the app or proxying
# to the API, so BOTH must be set — docker-compose.yml reads them for the client
# and API services.  setup.sh generates a memorable per-install password so no two
# demos share a default; if you are editing this file by hand, choose your own.
# Leaving either one empty DISABLES the login completely and serves the app to
# anyone who can reach the port (the container logs a warning saying so).
# For anything beyond a local/loopback demo, also read SECURITY.md.
ODIN_AUTH_USER=odin
ODIN_AUTH_PASSWORD=change-me

# ── CORPORATE NETWORK / SSL INSPECTION ────────────────────────────────────────
# If your network intercepts HTTPS with a corporate CA certificate, place the
# cert (PEM format; single cert or multi-cert bundle both work) in the ca/
# folder — no env var needed. odin.config auto-discovers any *.cer/*.crt/*.pem
# file there at runtime and injects it into all pipeline containers.
# Only set ODIN_CA_CERT if your cert is stored outside the ca/ folder:
# ODIN_CA_CERT=/path/to/your/corporate-ca.cer

# ── RECOMMENDED ON WINDOWS/WSL2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set ODIN_WORK_DIR to a WSL2-native Linux path to avoid I/O failures when
# Nextflow task containers access work directories on a Windows filesystem.
# docker-compose.yml mounts this path identically (host:container) so that
# sibling pipeline containers can reach it via the Docker socket.
# Docker auto-creates the directory on first launch — no mkdir needed.
# Default when unset: /tmp/odin_work (also auto-created).
# ODIN_WORK_DIR=/home/<your-wsl-user>/odin_work

# ── MINKNOW DATA OUTSIDE THE PIPELINE ROOT ────────────────────────────────────
# By default MinKNOW run folders are read from $ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT/minknow.
# If your sequencing data lives elsewhere (e.g. an external field drive like
# /mnt/<drive>/nanopore_sequencing_data), set the absolute WSL2/Linux path here —
# docker-compose.yml bind-mounts it identically (host:container) so pipeline
# sibling containers can read it too. No compose edit needed.
# The env var overrides any value set in the Settings UI (shown there as
# read-only, source "env") and survives database resets.
# ODIN_MINKNOW_DIR=/mnt/<drive>/nanopore_sequencing_data

# ── DATABASES OUTSIDE THE PIPELINE ROOT ───────────────────────────────────────
# By default Kraken2/Bracken databases are read from $ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT/databases.
# Set this to keep them elsewhere — recommended on WSL2: a fast ext4 path like
# /ODIN/databases, because Kraken2 memory-maps the multi-GB hash.k2d and loading
# it from a 9p-mounted Windows/USB drive is painfully slow.
# docker-compose.yml bind-mounts the path identically (read-only); no compose
# edit needed. Database entries with relative db_path values resolve against
# this directory; auto-discovery scans it for subdirs containing hash.k2d.
# ODIN_DATABASE_PATH=/ODIN/databases
# Optional alternative to the Databases page: manage database definitions in a
# CSV file (columns: tool, db_name, db_params, db_path) and point to it here.
# A present file takes priority over Databases-page entries and auto-discovery.
# Most installs need NEITHER — databases under ODIN_DATABASE_PATH are
# auto-discovered.
# ODIN_DATABASES_FILE=/path/to/pipeline/input_sheets/databases.csv

# ── CONTAINER RUNTIME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Socket the API container uses to spawn pipeline tool containers (DooD).
# Unset -> Docker Engine (/var/run/docker.sock), the default.
# For ROOTLESS PODMAN set it to Podman's Docker-compatible socket — the path
# contains your numeric user id (id -u). Also set ODIN_UI_PORT below, and see
# the Podman section in TROUBLESHOOTING.md for the one-time host setup.
# ODIN_CONTAINER_SOCK=/run/user/1000/podman/podman.sock

# ── UI PORT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host port for the web app (default 80 → http://localhost). Override when
# port 80 is taken, or when running rootless podman (which cannot bind ports
# below 1024 — 8099 works well):
# ODIN_UI_PORT=8099

# ── OPTIONAL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ODIN_TMP_DIR=/var/tmp
# Pipeline logs (+ .nextflow.log) outside the pipeline root. docker-compose.yml
# bind-mounts the path identically; no compose edit needed. Default when unset:
# alongside the pipeline outputs under ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT.
# ODIN_LOG_DIR=/home/<your-wsl-user>/odin_logs
# ODIN_STORE_DIR=/home/<your-wsl-user>/odin_store
# TAXPROFILER_REVISION=1.2.6
# NEXTFLOW_CONFIG_FILE=/app/config/odin.config
# SQUIRREL_IMAGE=articnetworkorg/squirrel:1.3.2
# Enlighten feather output outside the pipeline root (default:
# $ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT/enlighten/data). Mounted identically for the writer and
# read-only into the Enlighten API.
# ODIN_ENLIGHTEN_DATA_PATH=/path/to/odin-demo/pipeline/enlighten/data
# Local nf-core/taxprofiler checkout for offline/field use — the backend then
# runs $TAXPROFILER_DIR/taxprofiler from disk instead of pulling from GitHub.
# Must live under an identically-mounted path (e.g. inside ODIN_PIPELINE_ROOT).
# See FIELD_DEPLOYMENT.md.
# TAXPROFILER_DIR=/path/to/odin-demo/pipeline/nf
